[15.783352, "o", "printf '%s\\n' '--- review_protocol.md ---'; nl -ba /app/input/review_protocol.md | sed -n '1,220p'\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[15.783767, "o", "--- review_protocol.md ---\r\n"]
[15.790521, "o", "     1\t# Review protocol — letter to opposing counsel\r\n     2\t\r\n     3\tGoverns every line in `letter_lines.csv`, weighed against `original_instruction.md` and\r\n     4\t`clarification.md`.\r\n     5\t\r\n     6\t## RP-401 Which record prevails\r\n     7\t\r\n     8\tOn any subject the clarification speaks to, the clarification prevails. If the original instruction and the clarification take different positions on a subject, the clarification wins and the original falls away, no matter which entry the draft cites or how plainly the original was put: what the client said last is what the client instructs. For a line on a subject the clarification speaks to, the governing entry is always the clarification entry and never the original one (`RP-401`).\r\n     9\t\r\n    10\t## RP-402 Subjects the clarification repeats\r\n    11\t\r\n    12\tIf the clarification repeats a subject in the very terms the original used, the clarification entry nonetheless governs the line, and the line is weighed against it (`RP-402`).\r\n    13\t\r\n    14\t## RP-"]
[15.790718, "o", "403 Subjects only the original speaks to\r\n    15\t\r\n    16\tIf the clarification leaves a subject alone, the original entry on that subject governs and the line is weighed against it (`RP-403`).\r\n    17\t\r\n    18\t## RP-404 Subjects neither record speaks to\r\n    19\t\r\n    20\tIf neither record speaks to a subject, nothing governs: the line's verdict is NOT_IN_RECORD and its record entry is `NONE`, however reasonable the line may read (`RP-404`). A cited_entry that exists nowhere does not by itself make a line NOT_IN_RECORD — the governing entry is determined by the line's subject under RP-401 and RP-403, not by the cited_entry. NOT_IN_RECORD applies only when the subject itself is one that neither the original instruction nor the clarification addresses.\r\n    21\t\r\n    22\t## RP-405 How a line is weighed\r\n    23\t\r\n    24\tWeighed against its governing entry, a line is VERIFIED when the position it takes is the entry's own, and AT_ODDS when the entry's position differs from it — the reverse, something narrower, or "]
[15.791123, "o", "a different person (`RP-405`).\r\n    25\t\r\n    26\t## RP-406 The review table\r\n    27\t\r\n    28\t`record_entry` holds the id of the entry that governs the line, spelled as the records spell it, or `NONE` (`RP-406`).\r\n    29\t\r\n    30\t\r\n    31\t## RP-407 Cross-subject citation exception\r\n    32\t\r\n    33\tNotwithstanding RP-401, when a line's `cited_entry` is a clarification entry (CL-…) that speaks to a subject different from the line's own `subject`, the original instruction entry for the line's `subject` governs — not the clarification entry for the line's subject. The line is weighed against that original instruction entry, and the `record_entry` column reports the original instruction entry, not the clarification. (RP-407)\r\n    34\t\r\n    35\tThis applies only when the cited_entry is a clarification entry for a *different* subject. When the cited_entry is a clarification entry for the *same* subject, RP-401 applies normally. When the cited_entry is an original entry or NONE, RP-401 and RP-403 apply as before.\r\n  "]
[15.791282, "o", "  36\t\r\n    37\t## RP-408 Compound positions\r\n    38\t\r\n    39\tWhen a governing entry's position has two or more required limbs (for example, a marking and a tone requirement), a line that asserts only one limb and drops the others takes something narrower than the entry's own position, and is AT_ODDS (`RP-408`). The line is VERIFIED only when it takes the entry's full position, including every required limb.\r\n    40\t\r\n    41\t## Figures\r\n    42\t\r\n    43\t`verified_count` is the number of lines whose verdict is VERIFIED.\r\n    44\t`at_odds_count` is the at odds count: the lines whose verdict is AT_ODDS.\r\n    45\t`not_in_record_count` is the number of lines whose verdict is NOT_IN_RECORD.\r\n    46\t`clarification_governed_count` is the number of lines whose record entry is a clarification entry (`CL-…`).\r\n    47\tIn the answer, `Letter lines at odds with the record` is the number of lines whose governing entry takes a position\r\n    48\tother than the line's.\r\n"]
[15.792107, "o", "\u001b[?2004h\u001b[01;32mappuser@2cc695c72b41\u001b[00m:\u001b[01;34m/app\u001b[00m$ "]
[16.192601, "i", "printf '%s\\n' '--- clarification.md ---'; nl -ba /app/input/clarification.md | sed -n '1,220p'\n"]
[16.193484, "o", "printf '%s\\n' '--- clarification.md ---'; nl -ba /app/input/clarification.md | sed -n '1,220p'\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[16.193705, "o", "--- clarification.md ---\r\n"]
[16.19767, "o", "     1\t# Clarification — the client's later message\r\n     2\t\r\n     3\tWhat the client added afterwards, one entry per subject. The subject shown on each entry is the value the draft's `subject` column uses; any subject missing here is one the clarification leaves alone.\r\n     4\t\r\n     5\t## CL-301 `addressee`\r\n     6\t\r\n     7\tTo be clear: Adam is the father. The letter goes to Catherine's attorney — the mother's attorney — not to Adam's.\r\n     8\t\r\n     9\t## CL-302 `request`\r\n    10\t\r\n    11\tThe request stands: neither child overnight in her care unless the father is present.\r\n    12\t\r\n"]
[16.198126, "o", "    13\t## CL-303 `enclosure`\r\n    14\t\r\n    15\tYes, enclose the certified copy of the custodial plan.\r\n    16\t\r\n    17\t## CL-304 `salutation`\r\n    18\t\r\n    19\tOpen with Dear Counsel, since the attorney's name is still to be confirmed.\r\n    20\t\r\n    21\t## CL-305 `copies`\r\n    22\t\r\n    23\tDo not copy anyone on it.\r\n    24\t\r\n    25\t## CL-306 `delivery`\r\n    26\t\r\n    27\tSend it by post to counsel's office.\r\n    28\t\r\n    29\t## CL-307 `tone`\r\n    30\t\r\n    31\tUse a firm, formal and brief tone — not soft, and not long-winded.\r\n    32\t\r\n    33\t## CL-308 `behavioural_plan`\r\n    34\t\r\n    35\tThe plan is about confidence, stability and peer relations. The stays have set back confidence only — not the whole plan.\r\n    36\t\r\n    37\t## CL-309 `concern`\r\n    38\t\r\n    39\tI am concerned only about the position she has been put in. Do not raise whether she appreciates that the stays violate the order.\r\n    40\t\r\n    41\t## CL-310 `interim_contact`\r\n    42\t\r\n    43\tInterim contact arrangements must address all three aspects of th"]
[16.198382, "o", "e behavioural plan together — confidence, stability and peer relations — because interim contact affects all three. This entry takes precedence over CL-308 for any line whose subject is interim_contact, even when the line's wording addresses the behavioural plan or the stays.\r\n    44\t\r\n    45\t## CL-311 `without_prejudice`\r\n    46\t\r\n    47\tLines marked 'without prejudice' are settlement offers and must use a soft, conciliatory tone — the opposite of the firm tone the clarification requires for the rest of the letter. This entry takes precedence over CL-307 for any line whose subject is without_prejudice, even when the line's wording addresses the tone of the letter.\r\n"]
